### Page 4 of 6

```text
Board of County Commissioners
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
January 21, 2025
11. PUBLIC HEARING regarding a request for the vacation of a portion of
the existing 80-ft of the public right-of-way of Knowlwood Rd, as
identified on exhibit A.
25 - 039
Viewer's Report, Knowlwood Rd, RP# 513
Attachments:
Molly Bennett, Public Works, and Scott Larson, appointed viewer, reported to the
board that they recommend denying the petition for public health, safety, and welfare
reasons. Larson stated that under Laramie County Land Use Regulations,
subdivisions with 12 or more lots are required to have secondary access for
emergencies.  He noted there are plans to develop vacant lots and the existence of a
floodplain underscores the need for secondary access.  He said it is his
understanding that property owners and developers want to build a road and there are
advantages to having two accesses.  He informed that if the road were to be vacated,
it would be difficult to get the right-of-way back in the future.
Acting Chairman Thompson said he received a public comment from Steven
Olmstead, who opposed the vacation and contended public notice was not timely
received. Acting Chairman Thompson asked Bennett about the notification process.
She said notices were published and sent per state statute and included both a notice
in the Wyoming Tribune Eagle and letters sent to property owners within a 1000-ft
radius.
Curtis Mack, applicant, spoke in favor of the vacation and stated the road would cost
around $2 million to build. Mack said other access points would be better and cost
less.
Acting Chairman Thompson opened the hearing for PUBLIC COMMENT. Levi Martin,
resident, spoke at length in opposition to the vacation. He said the flood issue was
inconsequential and there are other emergencies that necessitate a secondary
access.  He echoed Larson's statement that if access is removed through the
vacation process, we will never get it back.  Georgia Spicer, resident, spoke in favor
of the vacation.  An email comment from Robert Downey, resident, was in favor of the
vacation. Hearing no further PUBLIC COMMENT, Acting Chairman Thompson closed
the hearing.
A motion was made by Commissioner Heath to approve the vacation. The
motion failed for lack of second.
A motion was made by Commissioner Zwonitzer, seconded by
Commissioner Hollingshead, to deny the request to vacate.
Commissioner Heath said she agreed with the viewer's report, and she did not want
to landlock parcels because the easement already exists.
Commissioner Zwonitzer discussed the safety issue if the road was vacated and
homes were built on the northern property of the two parcels.
Page 4 of 6
```

